import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const indCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: 'Claim: 1 + 2 + … + n = n(n+1)/2', note: 'To prove for every n, imagine the statements lined up like dominoes.' },
    { math: 'Base: n = 1 → 1 = 1·2/2 ✓', note: 'Knock over the first domino: verify the claim directly for n = 1.' },
    { math: 'Assume true for n = k', note: 'Suppose domino k falls: 1 + … + k = k(k+1)/2. This is the inductive hypothesis — a legitimate assumption for one step.' },
    { math: 'Then (k+1)(k+2)/2 ✓', note: 'Add k+1 to both sides and rearrange: the sum to k+1 equals (k+1)(k+2)/2. Domino k+1 falls — so every domino falls.' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/math/advanced/logic-and-proof">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Beyond computation, mathematics is the craft of <strong>proof</strong> — arguments airtight
    enough to survive all scrutiny, forever. Four proof patterns cover most of the ground: direct
    proof, proof by contrapositive, proof by contradiction, and mathematical induction. Each is
    a reusable frame; learning to spot which fits is the skill.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="mathematical induction — the domino argument, run on the oldest formula in the book" widget="equationStep" config={indCfg} />

  <h2>The four frames</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Direct:</strong> start from known facts, chain implications to the claim. Most algebra proofs.</li>
    <li><strong>Contrapositive:</strong> to prove "if A then B", prove "if not B then not A" — logically identical, often far easier. ("If n² is even, n is even" is contrapositive work.)</li>
    <li><strong>Contradiction:</strong> assume the claim is false, derive nonsense, conclude it was true. Euclid's proof that primes never run out works this way — assume a largest prime, multiply all of them, add one, and watch a new prime appear.</li>
    <li><strong>Induction:</strong> for claims about every natural number — base case plus a step that preserves truth. The domino guarantee above.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What separates proof from persuasion</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Examples never prove; one counterexample always disproves.</strong> A million even numbers don't make "all primes are odd" true — the number 2 is a one-line demolition.</li>
    <li><strong>Definitions are load-bearing.</strong> Most confusion in mathematics is two people using one word with different definitions.</li>
    <li><strong>Proofs explain, not just verify.</strong> A good proof shows <em>why</em> — that's why mathematicians hunt for new proofs of old theorems.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Hammack, Book of Proof; Polya, How to Solve It.</p>
</TopicPage>
